### What is Jackson @JsonView?

JsonView is a Jackson feature that lets you control which fields are serialized per endpoint. You define views as marker interfaces, annotate each field with the views it belongs to, and activate a view on the controller method. Jackson serializes only the fields tagged for the active view.

```java
package com.bank.view;

public class Views {
    public interface Summary {}
    public interface Detail extends Summary {}   // includes Summary fields
    public interface Admin extends Detail {}     // includes Detail + Summary
}
```

### How do you tag fields with views?

Each field is annotated with `@JsonView` referencing the view where it first appears. A field tagged `Summary` appears in all three views due to the hierarchy. A field tagged `Admin` appears only in the admin view.

```java
package com.bank.dto;

import com.bank.view.Views;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonView;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AccountView {
    @JsonView(Views.Summary.class) private String accountId;
    @JsonView(Views.Summary.class) private String holderName;
    @JsonView(Views.Summary.class) private BigDecimal balance;
    @JsonView(Views.Detail.class)  private String branchCode;
    @JsonView(Views.Detail.class)  private String ifscCode;
    @JsonView(Views.Admin.class)   private int internalRiskScore;
    // getters and setters
}
```

### How do you activate a view on the controller?

You annotate the controller method with `@JsonView(Views.Summary.class)` and Jackson serializes only the matching fields.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.dto.AccountView;
import com.bank.view.Views;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonView;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/accounts")
public class AccountController {

    @GetMapping("/{id}/summary")
    @JsonView(Views.Summary.class)
    public AccountView summary(@PathVariable String id) {
        return accountService.getView(id);
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}/detail")
    @JsonView(Views.Detail.class)
    public AccountView detail(@PathVariable String id) {
        return accountService.getView(id);
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}/admin")
    @JsonView(Views.Admin.class)
    public AccountView admin(@PathVariable String id) {
        return accountService.getView(id);
    }
}
```

```text
// GET /api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/summary
{ "accountId": "SAV2001", "holderName": "Rajesh", "balance": 150000.00 }

// GET /api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/admin
{ "accountId": "SAV2001", "holderName": "Rajesh", "balance": 150000.00,
  "branchCode": "NBI-001", "ifscCode": "NBI0000001", "internalRiskScore": 42 }
```

### How do you choose the view dynamically at runtime?

When the view depends on the caller's role rather than the endpoint, wrap the object in `MappingJacksonValue` and call `setSerializationView` with the chosen view class.

```java
import org.springframework.http.converter.json.MappingJacksonValue;

@GetMapping("/{id}")
public MappingJacksonValue get(@PathVariable String id) {
    AccountView account = accountService.getView(id);
    MappingJacksonValue wrapper = new MappingJacksonValue(account);

    if (SecurityContextHolder.getContext().getAuthentication()
            .getAuthorities().stream().anyMatch(a -> a.getAuthority().equals("ROLE_ADMIN"))) {
        wrapper.setSerializationView(Views.Admin.class);
    } else {
        wrapper.setSerializationView(Views.Summary.class);
    }
    return wrapper;
}
```

### How does JsonView reduce DTO explosion?

Without views, you might create `AccountSummaryResponse`, `AccountDetailResponse`, and `AccountAdminResponse`, three classes that are mostly the same fields plus three mappers. With JsonView, one class and tagging produce all three subsets.

```java
// Without JsonView – three near-identical DTOs:
record AccountSummaryResponse(String accountId, String holderName, BigDecimal balance) {}
record AccountDetailResponse(String accountId, String holderName, BigDecimal balance,
                              String branchCode, String ifscCode) {}
record AccountAdminResponse(String accountId, String holderName, BigDecimal balance,
                             String branchCode, String ifscCode, int internalRiskScore) {}

// With JsonView – one class, three views:
// (see AccountView above)
```

### When is a dedicated DTO still better?

JsonView can only include or exclude existing fields. It cannot reshape, rename, compute, or combine data from multiple sources. A dedicated DTO is better in four cases.

| Scenario | JsonView | Dedicated DTO |
|---|---|---|
| Same fields, different visibility | Ideal | Overkill |
| Computed values (e.g. available balance) | Cannot express | Clean |
| Different structure or nesting | Cannot express | Required |
| Combining multiple models | Cannot express | Required |

```java
// This needs a dedicated DTO – combines Account + Customer + Transactions:
public record DashboardResponse(
        String accountId,
        String customerName,
        String customerEmail,
        BigDecimal balance,
        BigDecimal availableBalance,     // computed: balance - holds
        List<TransactionSummary> recent  // from a different entity
) {}
// No single JsonView-tagged class can express this.
```

### What are common mistakes with JsonView?

A common mistake is using JsonView when the shapes genuinely differ structurally. Forcing views to reshape data they cannot handle leads to awkward workarounds. Another mistake is forgetting that fields without any `@JsonView` annotation are included in all views by default, which can accidentally expose fields.

```java
// Wrong – field without @JsonView is always serialized:
public class AccountView {
    @JsonView(Views.Summary.class) private String accountId;
    private String secretNote;  // no annotation → serialized in EVERY view!
}

// Correct – tag every field explicitly or set DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION to false:
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer configures `MapperFeature.DEFAULT_VIEW_INCLUSION` to `false` in the ObjectMapper so that unannotated fields are excluded by default, which is safer. They use JsonView for the narrow case of the same fields in overlapping subsets, especially admin versus public views. They reach for dedicated DTOs when the structure, computed values, or data sources genuinely differ. And they never apply `@JsonView` directly to JPA entities, instead using it on a dedicated view class that is populated by a service-layer mapper, keeping persistence and serialization concerns separate.
